prompts.user_template:
Score this paper for general-purpose LLM/foundation-model training and inference efficiency research. Use title and abstract only.

First check all required gates: (1) the optimized workload is model training, inference, or serving; (2) the main result includes measured resource savings or speedup; (3) the paper contributes a reusable mechanism applicable beyond a single vertical application, modality-specific task, robot workflow, dataset, or named model. For gate 3, state the transferable mechanism and the general bottleneck it solves. A named model or narrow experiment alone is not grounds for rejection if the mechanism itself is general. Do not assume that a cache, runtime, quantizer, speculative decoder, or scheduler is general simply because it has a familiar name: examine what inputs, model internals, or task repetition its benefit requires. Multiple experiments on the same vertical task do not demonstrate generality.

In scope: reusable LLM serving, scheduling, KV/context/prefix caching, offloading, distributed training, communication optimization, decoding, quantization, sparsity, compilers and runtimes for executing model workloads, and efficient training methods, if they pass all three gates. Edge or constrained-device systems are welcome when their bottleneck and method transfer to general-purpose model workloads. A method may trade some quality for substantial efficiency. The main contribution must be efficiency, not improved accuracy or reasoning. A privacy/security protocol remains out of scope when its resource gains are only relative to another privacy/security protocol, without a generally reusable improvement to ordinary model execution.

Out of scope even with measured acceleration: an application-specific adaptation of existing LLM techniques to robotics, healthcare, biology, finance, audio understanding, or another vertical; optimization relying on a domain's unique task structure or repeated trajectories; an implementation tailored to architectural features exclusive to one named model without a broader method. Speech or vision foundation-model work may pass only when the abstract establishes that its efficiency mechanism also applies to general-purpose foundation-model/LLM workloads. An LLM used to build a compiler, generate or search GPU kernels, write code, find bugs, or operate an application is out of scope: faster generated code does not change the paper's main contribution into model-execution infrastructure. Independently specified compilers/runtimes that accelerate model execution can pass. Also score low: accuracy-only methods, vertical assistants, benchmark-only or deployment-feasibility studies, hardware-first circuit/chip design, and unrelated CNN/GNN/recommender systems unless a transferable model-workload mechanism is clear.

Base score (0-10): 0-2 unrelated/application-only; 3-5 missing or uncertain evidence for one required gate, narrow transferability, or borderline; 6-8 clear reusable mechanism with measured benefits for general-purpose LLM/foundation-model training or inference; 9-10 exceptional and broadly reusable. If any required gate fails, base_score must be at most 5.

Preference bonus (0-3): only if base_score >= 6, add +1 for mild edge/resource-constrained relevance, +2 for clear on-device/edge inference efficiency, +3 for a central and broadly reusable on-device/NPU efficiency method under strong constraints. If base_score < 6, preference_bonus must be 0. Never use a bonus to rescue a narrow application or model-specific adaptation.

Final score = base_score + preference_bonus. Pass iff final_score >= 6. In the reason, mention the optimized workload, measured efficiency evidence, and why the mechanism does or does not transfer to general-purpose model workloads. Return JSON only:
{
  "paper_type": "one of: mlsys, ml_for_systems, ai_not_systems, pure_systems, borderline",
  "base_score": 0,
  "preference_bonus": 0,
  "final_score": 0,
  "pass": true,
  "reason": "2-4 sentences"
}

